            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Reforming the Carmelites</strong><br>\nMotivated by a desire to return to the original austerity and purpose of the Carmelite Order, Teresa began a movement for reform. She founded the Discalced Carmelites, a new order that upheld strict cloister and poverty. The reformed convents spread across Spain, despite opposition from church officials and local authorities who resisted changes to established practices.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Teresa's Written Works</strong><br>\nTeresa of Avila was a prolific writer; her most notable works include \"The Interior Castle\" and \"The Way of Perfection.\" These texts explore the inner life and the practice of prayer in achieving spiritual ecstasy. Her writings emphasize the importance of perseverance, love, and faith as means to a divine connection. Teresa's contribution to spiritual literature made her one of the foremost authorities on mystical theology.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Challenges and Perseverance</strong><br>\nTeresa faced numerous challenges, including skepticism and investigations by the Spanish Inquisition. Her mystical experiences were often questioned, with some accusing her of being influenced by the devil. Despite such accusations, she persisted with her reforms and her writing, which served to strengthen her arguments for a more devout and contemplative life.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
