          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Teresa's Influence and Legacy</strong><br>\nTeresa's work had a significant impact on Catholic spirituality and mysticism. Her emphasis on the personal and internal experience of God influenced not only her contemporaries but also future generations. She is considered a spiritual mother by the Carmelites and is often cited for her deep psychological insights into the human soul and its relationship with the divine.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Canonization and Recognition</strong><br>\nTeresa of Avila was canonized as a saint by Pope Gregory XV in 1622, just forty years after her death. Her canonization was a recognition of her holiness and the miraculous events attributed to her intercession. She was later declared a Doctor of the Church by Pope Paul VI in 1970, an honor given to saints who have significantly influenced the theology of the Church.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Teresa's Continuing Relevance</strong><br>\nThe teachings of Teresa of Avila remain relevant today. Her insistence on the importance of personal prayer and reflection resonates with many modern spiritual seekers. Her works are studied not only for their religious significance but also for their psychological depth, providing a guide for those seeking to explore the spiritual dimensions of human experience.</p>\n"
              },
